# UI primitives (CVA)

When introducing or modifying reusable UI structure, presentation, or interaction, identify the canonical owner before creating new styling or components. Skip this for business-logic, data, or copy-only changes.

1. Which existing primitive or composition owns this visual responsibility? Check the registry in [ui-architecture.md](../../packages/ui/docs/ui-architecture.md).
2. Does the requested behavior need a new variant, a new composition, or a genuinely new primitive?
3. Will the change introduce consumer-owned chrome that should belong to the primitive?
4. Can the result be inspected and modified through semantic classes?
5. Are there existing consumers that should receive the change automatically?

A primitive follows this layout:

```text
index.ts          # barrel re-export
<name>.variants.ts # CVA prop mapping; co-located <name>.css for semantic control CSS (see semantic-control-css.md)
<name>.tsx         # component (add 'use client' or use <name>.client.tsx if interactive)
<name>.stories.tsx # CSF3 story
<name>.types.ts    # optional shared types
```

- Migrated buttons and form controls emit semantic `ui-*` classes from co-located
  `*.css` (`@layer components`); CVA maps props to those classes. Other primitives
  still keep Tailwind in `*.variants.ts` until migrated.
- Use design-token classes only; never hardcode colors or font sizes.

Detail: [packages/ui/README.md](../../packages/ui/README.md).
